<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrrho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 360–c. 270 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pyrrho of Elis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyrrho of Elis was a Greek philosopher regarded as the founder of scepticism. He travelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s army as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he reportedly met ascetic sages. Teaching that the nature of things cannot be known, he recommended suspending judgement to reach tranquillity. He wrote nothing; his ideas survive through his pupil Timon and the later Pyrrhonist school.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrrho.jpg" alt="Pyrrho" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Girolamo Olgiati · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrrho_Heliensis_-_Illustrium_philosophorum_et_sapientum_effigies_ab_eorum_numistatibus_extractae.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
